\title{Productivity tools}

\p{Here are some of the tools I use for keeping track of things and staying productive.}

\ul{
  \li{[Roam Research](https://roamresearch.com)}
  \li{[Beeminder](http://www.beeminder.com/)}
  \li{[anki](http://ankisrs.net/)}
  \li{[LeechBlock](http://www.proginosko.com/leechblock.html)}
  \li{[todournament](https://alltom.github.io/todournament/)}
  \li{[Forester](https://www.forester-notes.org/index/)}
}

\p{I've been particularly influenced in my thinking and practice by [Cal Newport's](http://calnewport.com/blog/) [\em{Deep Work}](http://calnewport.com/books/deep-work/), David Allen's [\em{Getting Things Done}](http://gettingthingsdone.com/), and Mark Forster's [Final Version](http://archive.constantcontact.com/fs004/1100358239599/archive/1109980854493.html) system.  All highly recommended if you're looking for ways to become more organized and productive (and especially if, like me, being organized and productive is not your natural tendency).}
